<ProjectLayout
  title="Hybrid infrastructure: AWS and OCI"
  description="Infrastructure architecture running across AWS and OCI, networking and compute decisions, and the cost analysis behind the migration."
  domain="cloud"
  caseId="01"
>
  <h2>Context</h2>
  <p>
    Infrastructure originally hosted on AWS, migrated to OCI (Oracle Cloud Infrastructure) while
    maintaining the same service levels for the teams depending on it — APIs, portal, RPAs, and
    whitelabel services. The challenge wasn't just "switching providers," it was keeping zero
    perceptible downtime during the transition and redesigning networking and compute decisions that
    didn't make sense simply copied from one provider to the other.
  </p>

  <h2>Architecture decisions</h2>
  <p>
    Kubernetes clusters (OKE) replacing the previous orchestration, VCNs and Network Security Groups
    redesigned to reflect OCI's network segmentation model (different from AWS's Security Groups
    model), and IAM policies rebuilt from scratch following the principle of least privilege —
    instead of replicating broad permissions that existed for convenience in the previous
    environment.
  </p>

  <h2>Cost analysis</h2>
  <p>
    After the migration, I formalized a comparative cost analysis to validate the decision with
    technical leadership — not just confirming savings, but identifying <strong>where</strong>{' '}
    they actually came from, and where the new provider cost more.
  </p>
  <p>
    The item-by-item breakdown (compute, network/egress, storage, managed database) showed a
    structural cost reduction — mostly explained by compute and network. One notable exception: the
    managed database service (MySQL HeatWave) showed higher cost than the previous equivalent, which
    required separate investigation into the billing model and charged OCIDs — including a
    discrepancy between the number of billing OCIDs and the number of database instances visible in
    the console, still under investigation.
  </p>

  <h2>Result</h2>
  <p>
    Migration completed with no perceptible incident for end users, with a documented structural
    cost reduction and IAM policies more secure than the source environment's — a benefit that
    wasn't in the original scope, but emerged naturally from rebuilding the infrastructure from
    scratch instead of just replicating it.
  </p>

  <p class="pt-4 text-sm text-muted/80">
    <strong>Note:</strong> values and system names were omitted for confidentiality. Happy to detail the
    methodology in an interview.
  </p>
</ProjectLayout>
